@#$#@#$#@
## WHAT IS IT?

An agent-based model of white-nose syndrome (WNS) in a hibernating bat colony, used to compare annual vaccination strategies. Each turtle is one bat flying in a 3D cave. The model tracks how the population changes over ten years as the disease spreads and a share of the colony is vaccinated once a year.

## HOW IT WORKS

One tick is one day and a month is 30 ticks. The colony cycles through three seasons: swarming (August-September), hibernation (October-April) and roosting (May-July).

Each bat is susceptible, exposed, infected or cured (immune). Two bats start out exposed. During hibernation, bats roost close together near the cave ceiling and susceptible bats within radius 1 of another bat can become exposed. Exposed bats become infected after a latent period, and infected bats die at a rate of 1/60 per day. All bats also face natural mortality (mean lifespan 8.5 years), and bats reproduce during roosting while the colony is below carrying capacity.

Once a year, at the start of the chosen month, a fraction of the bats that are not infected or already immune are vaccinated and become cured.

## HOW TO USE IT

1. Set NUMBER-OF-DRAWERS, the starting number of bats.
2. Choose VACCINATION-RATE, the fraction of eligible bats vaccinated each year.
3. Choose VACCINATION_MONTH, the month in which vaccination happens. The simulation calendar also starts in this month.
4. Press SETUP, then GO. The run stops after 3650 ticks (ten years).

Bat colours: white = susceptible, yellow = exposed, red = infected, green = vaccinated.

## THINGS TO NOTICE

The colony usually crashes within the first two years, then settles at a much lower level. How low depends on the vaccination settings and on chance, so repeat a setting several times before comparing it with another.

## CREDITS AND REFERENCES

Arsenii Herasymov, Parikshit Parsoya and Devran Turson. Mentors: Alex Capaldi and Laura Tipton, James Madison University.
